Q.2 what is stream
Q.2 What is a Stream in Node.js?

A Stream is an object that allows you to read or write data continuously in small chunks instead of loading the entire data into memory at once.

For example, if you have a 2GB video file:

❌ Without streams:

fs.readFile('video.mp4', (err, data) => {
    console.log(data);
});

The entire file is loaded into memory.

✅ With streams:

import fs from 'fs';

const readStream = fs.createReadStream('video.mp4');

readStream.on('data', (chunk) => {
    console.log('Received chunk:', chunk.length);
});

The file is read piece by piece, which is much more memory-efficient.

Types of Streams

Readable Stream – Read data

fs.createReadStream('file.txt');

Writable Stream – Write data

fs.createWriteStream('output.txt');

Duplex Stream – Read and Write

const socket = net.createConnection();

Transform Stream – Modify data while reading/writing

import zlib from 'zlib';
const gzip = zlib.createGzip();
Q.3 What is process.nextTick() and setImmediate()?

Both are used to schedule code execution asynchronously, but they run at different stages of the Event Loop.
1. process.nextTick()

It executes the callback immediately after the current operation completes, before the event loop continues.

console.log("Start");

process.nextTick(() => {
    console.log("nextTick");
});

console.log("End");

Output:

Start
End
nextTick

2. setImmediate()

It executes the callback during the Check Phase of the Event Loop.

console.log("Start");

setImmediate(() => {
    console.log("setImmediate");
});

console.log("End");

Output:
Start
End
setImmediate

